  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of the 13-year-old girl, including fever, headache, photophobia, nausea, neck rigidity, and a maculopapular rash, suggests viral meningitis. The cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis shows elevated white blood cell count with a predominance of lymphocytes (85%), which is characteristic of viral infections, while the normal glucose level and moderate protein elevation further support this. Given her recent summer camp exposure, Echovirus is a common cause of viral meningitis in children and aligns with the CSF findings. Other options like Listeria monocytogenes, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and Neisseria meningitidis are less likely due to the lymphocytic predominance in the CSF, which typically indicates a viral rather than bacterial etiology. Herpes simplex virus is also a possibility but is less common in this age group compared to Echovirus. Therefore, the most likely causal organism is Echovirus.",
